extends Control
## A random encounter triggered from the overworld, run as a real
## Initiative-ordered round loop via CombatEncounter (p.155-156).
##
## Combat Log: every action becomes a history entry (roll card(s) plus
## an optional short notice), newest at the TOP of a scrollable list —
## no more free-form prose you have to hunt through. The newest entry
## renders full-size; older ones render smaller and greyed out so the
## eye is drawn to what just happened, not buried history.
##
## Weapon choice determines melee vs ranged: whichever weapon is
## equipped when the fight starts governs whether "Attack" resolves as
## an Opposed melee Test or an unopposed ranged one — so equipping a bow
## or a wand before an encounter genuinely means opening the fight at
## range, unless already Surprised. There's no separate in-combat
## weapon-swap or positioning system, so this is a deliberate
## simplification rather than a full engagement/range model.
##
## Defending in melee: the player chooses HOW to defend (Dodge or Parry)
## before any dice are rolled — they see WHAT is attacking them (weapon,
## Qualities), never the outcome in advance. Additional Effort is still
## available on their own defensive roll while doing this.

## Per the request ("add hover over tooltips at all greyed out buttons
## to indicate why they are greyed out, like you did with Disengage"):
## the single most common disable reason across the whole action menu,
## reused everywhere it applies rather than re-typed at each site.
const ACTION_USED_TOOLTIP := "Your Action for this turn has already been used."
## Per the request ("make all combat log roll card 20% bigger, and make
## combat log text font 20% smaller"): passed as every roll/fumble/
## critical-wound card builder's own `size_scale` in
## _rebuild_history_display() below — see roll_card_builder.gd's own
## class-level comment for exactly what this scales and why it's scoped
## to this screen's Combat Log alone. COMBAT_LOG_NOTICE_FONT_LATEST/
## HISTORIC are the plain-text notice line's old 18/15 sizes scaled to
## 80% and rounded to the nearest point.
const COMBAT_LOG_CARD_SCALE := 1.2
const COMBAT_LOG_NOTICE_FONT_LATEST := 10   ## 18 * 0.8 = 14.4, rounded, then -2, then -2 again (field/social/wilderness encounter screen-wide scale-down, applied twice)
const COMBAT_LOG_NOTICE_FONT_HISTORIC := 8   ## 15 * 0.8 = 12.0, then -2, then -2 again (field/social/wilderness encounter screen-wide scale-down, applied twice)
## Per the request: Charging doesn't grant any extra movement of its
## own — it moves the character using whatever Move they actually have
## left this turn (movement_remaining), and its only mechanical effect
## is unlocking the +10 Charge bonus (Up in Arms p.136) on the attack
## that follows, provided the character actually closes at least this
## many squares to reach the target. See _target_in_charge_range.
##
## Per the follow-up request ("change the minimum Charge range
## requirement to the character's current Move value, including any
## modifiers"): no longer a flat constant — the floor is now
## player.get_movement() itself, read fresh each time
## _target_in_charge_range runs, so Fleet Footed, Crippled Leg,
## Encumbrance, and anything else that moves the character's own
## Movement all flow through automatically rather than needing this
## floor hand-tuned separately.
const XP_PER_KILL := 5
const ASSESS_SKILLS: Array[String] = ["Perception", "Intimidate", "Cool", "Charm", "Leadership"]
const TRICK_CONDITIONS: Array[String] = ["", "Ablaze", "Blinded", "Entangled"]
## Sentinel value used only inside _render_turn_order_panel()'s own
## `display` Array to mark where the "End of Round" separator row goes
## — never a real Character, so a plain `==` comparison against it is
## enough to distinguish it from every genuine entry in that Array.
const _END_OF_ROUND_MARKER := "__END_OF_ROUND__"

## Each Arcane Lore's own optional "Magic Colour" effect (p.245-256 —
## checked against each Lore's own intro text, not reconstructed):
## - Fire: "+1 Ablaze Condition on anyone targeted... unless they also
##   possess the Arcane Magic (Fire) Talent."
## - Death: "+1 Fatigued Condition to any living target... may only
##   ever have a single Fatigued Condition gained in this manner at
##   any one time" — modelled as "only if they don't already have
##   Fatigued at all," a reasonable simplification since this project
##   doesn't track a Condition stack's specific source.
## - Light: "inflict one Blinded Condition on those targeted... unless
##   they possess the Arcane Magic (Light) Talent."
## - Witchcraft: "you may inflict one Bleeding Condition on anyone
##   targeted by spells from the Lore of Witchcraft."
## The other Lores (Beasts, Heavens, Metal, Life, Shadows, Hedgecraft)
## have real effects too, but they're self-buffs, passive Damage/
## Armour modifiers, or otherwise not a simple "toggle a Condition onto
## the target" shape — a documented gap rather than forced in
## inaccurately.
const MAGIC_COLOUR_EFFECTS := {
	"Fire": {"condition": "Ablaze", "immunity_talent": "Arcane Magic (Fire)", "cap_one": false},
	"Death": {"condition": "Fatigued", "immunity_talent": "", "cap_one": true},
	"Light": {"condition": "Blinded", "immunity_talent": "Arcane Magic (Light)", "cap_one": false},
	"Witchcraft": {"condition": "Bleeding", "immunity_talent": "", "cap_one": false},
}
const HEALING_DRAUGHT := "Healing Draught"
## Cordelia's Apothecary consumables (see her own spec, quoted in full
## on the skills_row/​_prompt_incapacitated_turn button-build comments
## and on _on_use_faxtoryll/_on_use_vitality_draught/_on_use_salwort
## below).
const FAXTORYLL := "Faxtoryll"
const SALWORT := "Salwort"
const VITALITY_DRAUGHT := "Vitality Draught"
const MAX_HISTORY_ENTRIES := 30

## Shared by _member_light_radius()/_current_light_radius() below — the
## bright (vis=2, fully-lit) fog-of-war radius substituted in for an
## is_outdoor dungeon theme (currently only the Goblin Fort) whenever
## it's genuinely daylight (battle_is_dark == false), so a roofless fort
## reads as sunlit across its whole footprint instead of only as far as
## a party lantern reaches. Comfortably bigger than the Goblin Fort's
## own fixed 8x16 room (see generate_fixed_room()'s call site) so the
## whole thing reveals at once; cheap to over-radius since _light_bfs()
## still stops at real walls/closed doors either way.
const _OUTDOOR_DAYLIGHT_RADIUS := 30
## Per the request ("lets move on to the new Creature portraits, replace
## them all"): every bestiary creature's icon now comes from
## CreaturePortraits (scripts/core/creature_portraits.gd) — full-size,
## transparent-background AI-generated art, one per creature, the same
## treatment the Career portraits already got. The old MONSTER_ICONS_BY_
## TYPE dict of small procedurally-generated 24x24-supersampled-to-96x96
## silhouette sprites (one preload() per bestiary entry) is gone; see
## _monster_icon_for() below, now just a thin wrapper over
## CreaturePortraits.get_portrait_or_fallback(). MONSTER_ICON is kept as
## the last-resort fallback for the same reason it always was — genuinely
## never expected to be hit now that all 62 bestiary entries have real
## art, but a silent blank icon would be worse than the old red blob if
## a future creature is ever added without matching art.
const MONSTER_ICON := preload("res://assets/sprites/monster.png")   ## fallback for any creature without dedicated art

## Monster display names get a numeric suffix when there's more than one
## in an encounter ("Wild Boar 2") — strip that back off to match the
## icon lookup, which is keyed by the base creature type.
##
## A prior build also reused this to strip the number from decorative
## name tags/labels (top-strip panel, turn-order list, battle-map name
## tag) — reverted per a follow-up request, so those all show the full
## `character_name` (WITH the number) again, same as combat log
## narration and the Overcast extra-target picker always have. This
## helper now exists solely for _monster_icon_for()'s own sprite
## lookup below.
static func _display_name(character_name: String) -> String:
	var parts := character_name.split(" ")
	if parts.size() > 1 and parts[-1].is_valid_int():
		return " ".join(parts.slice(0, parts.size() - 1))
	return character_name

static func _monster_icon_for(display_name: String) -> Texture2D:
	var tex := CreaturePortraits.get_portrait(_display_name(display_name))
	if tex == null:
		tex = MONSTER_ICON
	return tex

